{
  "image_name": "2c484f91eed21f546f014ffc3194a189.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how the blood–brain barrier regulates drug entry into the brain, and how this control appears to change in brain tumours. In the healthy state, tightly joined endothelial cells form a selective barrier between blood and brain tissue. Drug molecules can be transported inward by uptake transporters or pumped back into the bloodstream by efflux transporters such as P-gp, so the balance of uptake versus efflux determines how much unbound drug reaches brain tissue and cerebrospinal fluid. Pericytes, basement membrane, and astrocyte endfeet are shown as supporting structures around the vessel.\n\nIn the tumour setting, the barrier appears disrupted and more permissive. The basement membrane is modified, immune and tumour cells are present around the vessel, and signalling factors such as VEGF and ANG2 are shown near their receptors. Tight junctions seem reduced, allowing more passive diffusion of drug molecules into the brain. The right side highlights this contrast with enlarged views of intact versus loosened junctions and a brain schematic indicating drug distribution into CSF and brain. Overall, the figure emphasizes that tumour-associated vascular changes can increase brain drug penetration compared with the normal blood–brain barrier.",
